{
  "abstract": "A degree-nine seven-clique in a biplanar graph fails to extend an exterior nine-coloring precisely when all its outside-neighborhood triples are rainbow on one common three-color palette. We give replacement theorems that handle multiple such boundary partitions simultaneously. If the distinct neighborhood triples are pairwise disjoint and at least three occur, component stars eliminate every obstruction in every planar-layer representation. For the overlap pattern $abc,bde,cfg$, disjoint private branches suffice whenever $abc$ occurs once, and for arbitrary multiplicities when both private layers are connected. Standalone certificates cover the remaining geometric cases using literal Kuratowski subdivisions and verified branch replacements. A fixed representation with multiplicities $(2,1,4)$ defeats every terminal-preserving rooted-minor replacement that deletes or absorbs all private vertices; retaining at most six private vertices does not help. Nevertheless a single private-edge transfer yields a reduction in every ambient extension of that allocation. Forbidden terminal-edge augmentations force a cut vertex, allowing the full exterior component to be reembedded safely. A nearby literal example shows why two-terminal attachments cannot be ignored. Finally, a 2-connected biplanar graph of minimum degree nine has a bad exterior coloring that resists every single Kempe interchange but admits an explicit two-interchange repair. These results distinguish component stars, smaller branches, fixed-layer minors, ambient-preserving transfers and recoloring. They exclude specified configurations from a minimum-order counterexample, but supply neither an unavoidable configuration family nor a ten-chromatic biplanar graph.",
